# nifty-executor — droplet side of the Fib bot

Runs on the droplet at **139.59.33.81**, whose IP is registered with Upstox. Only
this machine may place orders; the dashboard on Vercel has dynamic IPs and never
will.

```
token_receiver.py           catches the token pushed from the dashboard
upstox_orders.py            order placement — REAL MONEY
instruments.py              resolves the front-month Nifty future
test_order.py               one resting order, then cancel — the live proof
test_receiver.py            44 checks, no token needed
test_orders.py              29 checks, no token or network needed
fib-token-receiver.service  systemd unit
Caddyfile                   TLS termination (only if not using Tailscale Funnel)
```

## The executor

`executor.py` polls the dashboard's signal and keeps the exchange in the state
that signal implies. **It ships with `LIVE = False`** — it decides everything
exactly as it would live, reports each intended action prefixed 🧪, and places
nothing.

```bash
# /root/fib-bot/executor.env   (chmod 600)
APP_PIN=<the dashboard PIN — the droplet mints its own session to read the signal>
SIGNAL_URL=https://nse-dashboard-gamma.vercel.app/api/fib/signal
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=<bot token>
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=<your chat id>
TOKEN_PATH=/root/fib-bot/.upstox_token
EXECUTOR_STATE=/root/fib-bot/executor_state.json
```

```bash
cp fib-executor.service /etc/systemd/system/
systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl enable --now fib-executor
journalctl -u fib-executor -f
```

Telegram: `/status` (mode, position, signal, token), `/stop` (no new orders),
`/start`.

**Going live is one flag and a restart**, and deliberately nothing else:
`LIVE = True` at the top of `executor.py`, then
`systemctl restart fib-executor`. Do not do that until dry mode has run clean
for several sessions.

### What it refuses to do

- hold more than one position at a time
- open anything within 2 days of contract expiry
- trade while the token is missing, expired, or rejected
- place a MARKET order — banned over the API, so exits are LIMIT through the
  touch and the stop is an SL-M

### The orphaned-stop hazard

Upstox has no `reduce_only`. A protective stop is a plain opposite-side SL-M, so
one left resting after a position closes some other way **can open a short**. The
executor cancels any stop or target it finds while flat, and says so when it
does.

## Running the order test

The only deliberate real-money touch before the executor. It places one limit buy
5% below the market so it rests unfilled, reads it back, and cancels it.

```bash
cd /root/fib-bot
python3 test_order.py          # asks you to type PLACE before anything is sent
```

It refuses to run outside 09:15–15:30 IST Mon–Fri. An order placed while NSE is
shut becomes an after-market order that queues for the next session — it would
sit over a weekend and could open at Monday's bell, which is the opposite of a
test that takes no position. `--force` overrides that if you know why you want it.

It also aborts if the token's account is not `BOT_ACCOUNT_ID`.

Watch it in the Upstox app: a resting order appears far below price, then
disappears. Both should happen within a few seconds.

## Why a push, and why a domain

Upstox issues no refresh token and every access token dies at **03:30 IST**. So
each morning you log into the dashboard as the trading account and press *Sync
token to droplet*; the token travels once, over TLS, and lands here.

The token crosses the public internet, so it must be encrypted end to end. Caddy
gets a Let's Encrypt certificate automatically, but **Let's Encrypt will not
issue certificates for bare IP addresses** — so a hostname pointing at the
droplet is required. It does **not** have to be a purchased one; see below.

## Setup

### 1. The shared secret

```bash
openssl rand -hex 32
```

The same value goes in **both** places. Nowhere else — not in git, not in chat.

### 2. A hostname (free — do not buy a domain)

Use **DuckDNS**: sign in at [duckdns.org](https://duckdns.org) with GitHub or
Google, claim a subdomain, and set its IP to `139.59.33.81`. Two minutes, free
forever. Then:

```bash
dig +short nse-fib-bot.duckdns.org      # must print 139.59.33.81 before starting Caddy
```

If it doesn't resolve yet, Caddy's certificate request fails and it backs off
before retrying — so wait for DNS first.

A subdomain of a domain you already own is equally fine, and strictly nicer.

> **Do not use nip.io or sslip.io.** They are the obvious shortcut —
> `139.59.33.81.nip.io` already resolves to this droplet with no signup at all —
> but neither is on the [Public Suffix List](https://publicsuffix.org/list/),
> which means every subdomain of `nip.io` worldwide shares **one** Let's Encrypt
> rate-limit bucket of 50 certificates per week. It is effectively always spent,
> and the failure surfaces at certificate issuance rather than at DNS, so it
> reads like a Caddy problem and isn't. `duckdns.org` is on the list, so each
> subdomain gets its own bucket. (Verified against the live PSL — `duckdns.org`
> is present, `nip.io` and `sslip.io` are not.)

### 2b. Alternative: Tailscale Funnel, no open ports

If you'd rather not expose 80/443 at all, skip DuckDNS **and** Caddy:

```bash
curl -fsSL https://tailscale.com/install.sh | sh
tailscale up
tailscale funnel 8787
```

That publishes the receiver at `https://<machine>.<tailnet>.ts.net` with a
certificate Tailscale obtains and renews for you, over Tailscale's own network —
the droplet's firewall stays shut. Set `DROPLET_SYNC_URL` to that URL instead.
Same security posture (the endpoint is still public, so `BOT_SYNC_SECRET` is
still what protects it), fewer moving parts, one less service to keep patched.

### 3. Droplet

```bash
mkdir -p /root/fib-bot && cd /root/fib-bot
# copy token_receiver.py here

cat > /root/fib-bot/receiver.env <<'EOF'
BOT_SYNC_SECRET=<the openssl value>
BOT_ACCOUNT_ID=84BDRQ
TOKEN_PATH=/root/fib-bot/.upstox_token
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=<bot token>
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=<your chat id>
EOF
chmod 600 /root/fib-bot/receiver.env    # systemd reads it as root; nobody else should

cp fib-token-receiver.service /etc/systemd/system/
systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl enable --now fib-token-receiver
journalctl -u fib-token-receiver -f
```

Caddy:

```bash
apt install -y caddy
# put Caddyfile at /etc/caddy/Caddyfile, with your DuckDNS subdomain
ufw allow 80/tcp && ufw allow 443/tcp
systemctl reload caddy
curl https://nse-fib-bot.duckdns.org/health   # -> {"ok": true}
```

The receiver binds `127.0.0.1` only, so it is unreachable from the internet
except through Caddy. **Do not** change `BIND_HOST`, and do not open 8787 in the
firewall.

### 4. Vercel

Project Settings → Environment Variables, then **redeploy** (env changes don't
apply to an existing build):

| Variable | Value |
|---|---|
| `BOT_SYNC_SECRET` | the same openssl value |
| `DROPLET_SYNC_URL` | `https://nse-fib-bot.duckdns.org/sync-token` |
| `BOT_ACCOUNT_ID` | `84BDRQ` |

Do **not** add any of these to `next.config.js`'s `env` block — that inlines
values into the browser bundle.

### 5. Point OAuth at nifty-executor

The dashboard's login currently uses whichever Upstox app `UPSTOX_API_KEY`
names. To make *Sync* produce an order-capable token for your account, set in
Vercel:

| Variable | Value |
|---|---|
| `UPSTOX_API_KEY` | `538082d5-a8d7-4d1b-8fa7-54c27ea1c119` |
| `UPSTOX_API_SECRET` | the nifty-executor secret |
| `UPSTOX_REDIRECT_URI` | must match the redirect registered on that app |

This does **not** affect prices: those come from `UPSTOX_ANALYTICS_TOKEN`, and
`resolveAccessToken()` prefers it without ever reading the OAuth credentials.

## Verify

```bash
python3 test_receiver.py     # 30 checks, no token needed
```

Then the real round-trip:

1. Log into the dashboard **as the trading account** and connect Upstox.
2. Open `/fib`, press **Sync token to droplet**.
3. Telegram should say `✓ token synced for 84BDRQ, valid until 03:30 IST`.
4. On the droplet: `ls -l /root/fib-bot/.upstox_token` → exists, mode `600`.

A wrong-account login is refused with the account it actually found, and nothing
is written.

## Troubleshooting

**`status=200/CHDIR`, restart counter climbing** — the unit could not enter
`/root/fib-bot`. Caused by `ProtectHome=true`, which masks `/root`; the shipped
unit has it off for exactly this reason. Check it wasn't re-enabled.

**Starts, then fails only when a token is synced** — likely
`RestrictAddressFamilies` without `AF_UNIX`, which breaks DNS resolution inside
the sandbox, so the Upstox profile check can't resolve the hostname.

**`BOT_SYNC_SECRET is not set. Refusing to start.`** — `receiver.env` is missing,
empty, or unreadable. By design: a receiver with no lock is worse than one that
isn't running.

**502 through the public URL** — Funnel/Caddy is fine, the receiver isn't
running. `systemctl status fib-token-receiver`.

**`could not verify account`, journal shows Cloudflare error 1010** — Upstox is
behind Cloudflare, which rejects Python's default `User-Agent: Python-urllib/3.x`
on client signature before the request reaches Upstox. Tell-tale: `curl` from the
same droplet gets a normal 401 while Python gets 403 with Cloudflare HTML.
Fixed by sending an explicit `User-Agent` — see `USER_AGENT` in
`token_receiver.py`. Any Upstox call added later needs the same header.

**404 at the bare URL** — correct. Only `/health` and `/sync-token` answer.

## What refuses what

The dashboard checks the account before pushing; this server checks it **again**
from the registered IP. That is not redundancy for its own sake — the two fail
differently. Vercel's check can be bypassed by anything that can call the sync
endpoint directly; this one cannot be reached without the secret. Neither alone
is sufficient.

| Condition | Result |
|---|---|
| missing / wrong `X-Sync-Secret` | 403, body never read |
| body > 8 KB, malformed, or incomplete | 400 |
| token already past its expiry | 400 |
| Upstox can't confirm the account | 502 — unverified is refused, not assumed |
| token belongs to another account | 403, names the account found |
| all clear | 200, atomic write at mode 600, Telegram confirms |
